{"id": "live-300", "title": "Waterworks Operating Co., LLC v. 514 W. 24th Owner LLC (Mechanic's-Lien and Breach-of-Contract Judgment Partly Vacated on Appeal)", "category": "construction-defect", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-10-15", "jurisdiction": "Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of the State of New York, First Department", "state": "NY", "amount": "$1,396,058.74 judgment vacated and remitted for recalculation of damages", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/11035230/waterworks-operating-co-llc-v-514-w-24th-owner-llc/", "summary": "New York's Appellate Division, First Department, on October 15, 2026 affirmed liability but vacated a roughly $1.4 million mechanic's-lien and breach-of-contract judgment that bathroom-fixtures supplier Waterworks Operating Co. won against 514 West 24th Owner LLC over unpaid materials delivered to a Chelsea redevelopment project. The court rejected the Owner's argument that an unauthorized construction manager's change orders could not bind it, but found Waterworks' proof of $237,069 of the claimed damages improperly relied on a reply-only affidavit, and remitted the case to Supreme Court to recalculate damages.", "significance": "The ruling is a reminder to commercial developers and contractors that winning on liability in a mechanic's-lien or construction-payment dispute does not guarantee the damages figure survives appeal if any portion of the proof was submitted at the wrong procedural moment. It also signals that an owner's silent acceptance of materials delivered under a change order can ratify that change order even where a construction manager arguably lacked authority to approve it under the governing agreement.", "judge": null, "tags": ["mechanics-lien", "construction-payment", "change-orders", "chelsea", "new-york"]}
